## 2026-06-02 — Sprint 4 ISSUE-067 Transport Baseline and ISSUE-069 Projection Freshness Decision

**Date:** 2026-06-02

**Decision:**
Sprint 4 begins with ISSUE-067 Transport Baseline Audit.

ISSUE-067 establishes the transport baseline and verifies current polling-based realtime behavior.

ISSUE-069 Projection Freshness + Stale Refresh Triggers is approved and implemented as a bounded frontend-only refinement within the existing polling architecture.

The selected solution is:

* accelerated ACTIVE / ACTIVATING projection polling (500ms cadence)
* non-active projection polling remains 1000ms
* debounced event-informed projection refetch (200ms throttle) when new event batches arrive
* canonical projection truth still comes only from backend /projection/{workflow_id}
* events remain observability-only freshness hints and are never applied directly as projection truth

**Reason:**
ISSUE-067 found zero websocket infrastructure in the current codebase and no transport authority violations.

The actual problem was operator-visible projection freshness lag from independent event/projection polling loops, not a correctness defect.

Implementing websocket migration first would have been speculative and higher risk than refining the existing HTTP polling architecture.

A frontend-only polling/refetch refinement addressed the observed gap with lower architectural risk and preserved all existing authority boundaries.

**Architectural Clarifications:**

* polling remains legal and operational
* websocket/SSE remains deferred and not required for correctness
* projection freshness is visibility refinement only, not correctness architecture
* projection remains orchestrator-owned and non-authoritative
* frontend remains projection-derived and non-authoritative
* events remain observability-only
* event-informed refetch must never become event-driven projection application
* stale/freshness behavior must not synthesize lifecycle truth
* transport does not own lifecycle authority
* transport does not own projection authority
* transport does not own replay authority
* transport does not own recovery authority
* transport does not own retry authority
* transport does not own Task Hub / History semantics
* transport does not own FAILED actionability semantics
* transport does not own planning/replan semantics
* transport does not own mutation legality semantics

**Explicitly NOT Introduced:**

* no websocket migration
* no event sourcing
* no backend push system
* no backend projection authority change
* no projection schema change
* no lifecycle semantic change
* no recovery semantic change
* no replay semantic change
* no retry semantic change
* no replan semantic change
* no Task Hub / History change
* no FAILED actionability change
* no planning/replan change
* no mutation legality change
* no frontend lifecycle synthesis
* no projection-to-authority feedback

**Impact:**

* Operator-visible projection freshness during ACTIVE execution is improved within the existing polling architecture.
* Transport architecture remains stable and authority-safe.
* Websocket migration remains deferred until justified by future operational pressure.
* Sprint 4 transport/freshness work proceeds through bounded refinements rather than speculative infrastructure redesign.

**Status:**
ISSUE-067 COMPLETE + VERIFIED; ISSUE-069 IMPLEMENTED + BUILD-VALIDATED + PARTIAL MANUAL REGRESSION VALIDATION

